<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Brooke returns three months after his last visit, about six and a half months into tamoxifen. He has missed no doses; his pill organizer is empty on the right days. Hot flashes are unchanged, mild, two or three times a week. Asked today about sexual side effects, which were not covered at the last visit, he says his libido has been lower since starting tamoxifen; erections are adequate, and he is not troubled enough to want treatment. He has had no leg swelling or pain, chest pain, breathlessness, or change in vision, and no new lump or swelling on the chest wall or in either armpit. He met the genetic counselor four weeks ago: the multigene panel found no pathogenic variant in BRCA2, BRCA1, PALB2, CHEK2, ATM, or the other genes tested, and he was given a letter to share with his relatives. He takes vitamin D daily and walks most days. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tamoxifen</td><td>About six and a half months; no missed doses</td></tr><tr><td>Side effects</td><td>Mild hot flashes; lower libido, not bothersome; no clot symptoms</td></tr><tr><td>Genetics</td><td>No pathogenic germline variant; family letter given</td></tr><tr><td>Recurrence symptoms</td><td>None</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

